<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Statistical Significance Analysis</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The statistical significance of our model-level confidence metric is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Statistical Significance Analysis ‣ 5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ SID: Multi-LLM Debate Driven by Self Signals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(c) and Figure <a href="#A4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix D More Results ‣ SID: Multi-LLM Debate Driven by Self Signals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>–<a href="#A4.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ Appendix D More Results ‣ SID: Multi-LLM Debate Driven by Self Signals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>, where results for both the LLM (GPT-OSS-20B) and MLLM (LLaVA1.6-13B) are presented. In the figure, C and W denote correct and incorrect responses, respectively. Across two tasks of varying difficulty: GPQA and MMLUpro, our SID maintains a consistent confidence threshold within the correct group for the same model (e.g., NLL max <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 7.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>7.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 7.5</annotation></semantics></math>), highlighting the stability and robustness of our model-level confidence signal.</p>
</div>
